# Which test suites still run a case their own way, and how many times each.
#
# tests/testlib.ps is the one place a case that may raise is run, and the one
# place that puts the world back afterwards. A suite asks for it with
# `%!testlib` on its first line; run-ps-test.sh prepends it to those and to
# nothing else, because some suites here assert that a program's own
# dictionary starts empty or count the dictionary stack, and six read their
# own source through currentfile.
#
# WHAT IS COUNTED, and why it is not a list of names. Naming the suites that
# define a procedure called works, refuses, raises or outcome -- the shared
# framework's own names -- finds only the copies spelled like the shared one,
# and a runner is renamed as easily as it is written. `/erris` is a
# raises-clone under another name, defined in twenty-four suites here. A
# register derived by name shrinks when a runner is renamed rather than
# converted, and says nothing about a suite already on the framework that
# kept a runner of its own.
#
# What is counted now is `stopped`, the operator any such runner has to
# reach -- it is the only way a PostScript program catches an error -- so no
# choice of name can dodge it. Occurrences, not lines, because five lines
# here carry two; and an occurrence is counted only when no % precedes it on
# the line, so prose about the operator is not read as a use of it.
#
# The COUNT is part of the entry, and it is what makes this a measure rather
# than a list. A suite converted case by case has its count fall, so partial
# progress is visible; a suite that grows a new hand-rolled case fails even
# though it is already listed, which a list of names could not catch. The
# register is held to the tree in both directions, so a count can only be
# changed by changing the suite.
#
# A high count is not by itself a defect. Some of these suites have
# `stopped` as their SUBJECT -- the error dictionary's contents, stop and
# stopped themselves, what an error leaves on the stacks -- and those will
# end as `cannot` with the reason written down. None of them has been
# examined yet, so they are all `own`: not yet looked at, rather than judged
# convertible.
#
# TWO DISPOSITIONS. `own` is a suite that has not moved. `cannot` is one
# that must not move, and carries the reason: a suite whose subject is the
# state of a program's own dictionary cannot be handed a framework defined
# into that dictionary, because the framework is then what it reports.
# Without somewhere to say that, the only records of it would be a
# conversion that failed and whoever next tried the same thing.
#
# HOW A CONVERSION IS CHECKED, and what the check does not cover. A suite
# being moved is run through its own harness before and after and required to
# print the same bytes. That is a strong check on behaviour and no check at
# all on strength: a suite whose own runner fixed the expected error name
# inside itself, converted to a shared runner that accepts any error, goes on
# printing SUCCESS while asking a weaker question. So a conversion that
# changes which question a case asks must also show the case failing when the
# answer is wrong.
#
# The prose above ends at the two legend lines below, and the guard checks
# they are still there. A rewrite of this file that generates the register by
# finding the first `own` in it truncates the header at the word in `own
# source through currentfile` and merges the first entry onto the comment,
# which nothing reading only the uncommented lines can see.
#
# Regenerate with:  sh tests/check-testlib.sh . --derive
# which is the same derivation the check enforces, so the two cannot drift.
#
#   own    <suite>  <cases run its own way>
#   cannot <suite>  <cases run its own way>  <reason>

